{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SVmpQwtvDC9ZMndmXjob6Q","type":"tool_result","content":"usage: answer.py [-h] [--repo REPO] [--met {yes,no,unknown}] [--evidence EVIDENCE] [--quote QUOTE] [--counter COUNTER] [--note NOTE]\n                 [--rating {likely_pass,gap,needs_evidence,na_candidate}] [--confidence {high,medium,low}] [--summary SUMMARY] [--next NEXT_STEP] [--violates VIOLATES]\n                 [--also-affects ALSO_AFFECTS] [--verdict {confirmed,dismissed}] [--item ITEM] [--reason REASON]\n                 findings {check,flag,item,prescan,next} [target]\n\nWrite one answer into a findings file, check it, and show what's next.\n\nUse this instead of editing the JSON by hand.\n\n  Answer a check:\n    python answer.py FINDINGS.json check B05.2 --met no --evidence src/x.py:41 \\\n        --quote \"exact code on that line\" --note \"what the code does\" [--counter \"what you searched\"]\n\n  Add a red flag:\n    python answer.py FINDINGS.json flag B05 --evidence src/x.py:41 --note \"what's wrong\"\n\n  Finish an item:\n    python answer.py FINDINGS.json item B05 --rating gap --confidence high \\\n        --summary \"one sentence\" --next \"what to fix\" [--violates \"exact words from the requirement\"] \\\n        [--also-affects E06]\n\n  Resolve a pre-scan hit:\n    python answer.py FINDINGS.json prescan H003 --verdict confirmed --item R03 --reason \"...\"\n\n  Show the next unanswered check:\n    python answer.py FINDINGS.json next\n\nThe quote is checked against the file right away; a \"yes\" without a matching quote\nand a counter-search is refused. Paths are relative to the repo, which defaults to the\nfolder holding FINDINGS.json (set --repo to change it).\n\npositional arguments:\n  findings\n  {check,flag,item,prescan,next}\n  target\n\noptions:\n  -h, --help            show this help message and exit\n  --repo REPO\n  --met {yes,no,unknown}\n  --evidence EVIDENCE\n  --quote QUOTE\n  --counter COUNTER\n  --note NOTE\n  --rating {likely_pass,gap,needs_evidence,na_candidate}\n  --confidence {high,medium,low}\n  --summary SUMMARY\n  --next NEXT_STEP\n  --violates VIOLATES\n  --also-affects ALSO_AFFECTS\n  --verdict {confirmed,dismissed}\n  --item ITEM\n  --reason REASON\n{\n  \"id\": \"B11.1\",\n  \"check\": \"Container settings set non-root, no privilege escalation, all capabilities dropped, and a seccomp profile, matching Pod Security 'restricted' or the same controls.\",\n  \"coverage_required\": true,\n  \"verify\": \"1) Find pod `securityContext` and container `securityContext`, plus namespace labels. 2) Check `runAsNonRoot: true`, `allowPrivilegeEscalation: false`, `capabilities.drop: [ALL]`, `seccompProfile: RuntimeDefault`. 3) Answer no if any is missing on any container (including init and sidecars), set the wrong way, or overridden per container, or the Dockerfile runs as root with no override.\",\n  \"candidate_sites\": []\n}\n{\n  \"id\": \"B12.2\",\n  \"check\": \"CODEOWNERS or required-review rules cover the prompt, rules, and harness files.\",\n  \"coverage_required\": true,\n  \"verify\": \"1) Read `CODEOWNERS` and review rules (`.github/`, rulesets, branch protection IaC). 2) Check patterns match prompt, rules, and harness paths. 3) Answer no if those paths are uncovered, the pattern misses them (wrong glob, case), or no CODEOWNERS exists. Answer unknown if required reviews are set only in repo settings outside the code.\",\n  \"candidate_sites\": []\n}\n{\n  \"id\": \"R02.1\",\n  \"check\": \"Outside content (API responses, tool output, documents, connector messages) goes into user or tool message roles, never into the system or policy prompt.\",\n  \"coverage_required\": true,\n  \"verify\": \"1) Find where model requests are built (`system=`, `messages`, `role: system`, prompt templates). 2) Trace outside content (tool output, documents, API responses) into the request. 3) Answer no if any outside content is concatenated into the system or policy prompt, including retrieved context or tool descriptions from servers.\",\n  \"candidate_sites\": []\n}\n{'brace_checklist': '2026-10-01', 'repo': '', 'commit': None, 'reviewed_at': '2026-10-20T13:45:23Z', 'scope': \"TODO: what was reviewed and what wasn't.\", 'review_protocol': 'coverage-first-v1', 'findings': '', 'injection_attempts': [], 'prescan_resolutions': {}}\n['B11', 'B12', 'R01', 'R02', 'R03']\n{\n \"item\": \"B11\",\n \"gate\": \"G2\",\n \"title\": \"Minimal, isolated runtime\",\n \"requirement\": \"Remove shells, downloaders, package managers, mounts, and privileges the agent doesn't need. As a container baseline, meet the Kubernetes Pod Security Standards \\\"restricted\\\" profile or the same controls elsewhere. That means non-root, no privilege escalation, all capabilities dropped, and a seccomp profile set. Where supported, also use a read-only root filesystem and user namespaces; the restricted profile doesn't require those two. For running untrusted code, use a sandbox that fits the threat, such as a microVM or a user-space kernel. Ordinary containers share the host's kernel. For managed runtimes, record the provider's isolation evidence and what you can't see. Test that host access is blocked. A blocked probe does not prove the sandbox resists every escape.\",\n \"not_covered\": [\n  {\n   \"problem\": \"Container image not pinned by digest or signature-checked\",\n   \"owner\": \"B10\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"problem\": \"Outbound traffic to unapproved internet destinations\",\n   \"owner\": \"B09\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"problem\": \"Network path to admin systems or other tenants' resources\",\n   \"owner\": \"B08\"\n  }\n ],\n \"rating\": \"needs_evidence\",\n \"confidence\": \"low\",\n \"evidence\": [],\n \"checks\": [\n  {\n   \"id\": \"B11.1\",\n   \"check\": \"Container settings set non-root, no privilege escalation, all capabilities dropped, and a seccomp profile, matching Pod Security 'restricted' or the same controls.\",\n   \"verify\": \"1) Find pod `s",